.TH PRT-SNAPSHOT 1 "August 2026" "prt-snapshot 0.4" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
prt-snapshot \- manage snapshots of the package set installed by prt-get

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B prt-snapshot
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B prt-snapshot
stores the package names reported by
.B prt-get listinst
and can later compare the current installed package set with a stored snapshot
or restore package membership to that snapshot.

.PP
Snapshots are package-set snapshots, not full system snapshots. Package
versions, configuration changes, filesystem state outside package ownership,
runtime data, and the historical state of the ports tree are not captured.

.SH COMMANDS

.TP
.B clean
Remove all numeric snapshots managed by
.BR prt-snapshot .

.TP
.B store
.I msg
Store the current installed package set with the supplied message.

.TP
.B restore
.I num
Restore package membership to snapshot
.IR num .
Packages not present in the target snapshot are removed. Missing packages are
installed with
.BR "prt-get install" .

.TP
.B diff
.I num
Show package-set differences between the current system and snapshot
.IR num .

.TP
.B list
List stored snapshots in numeric order.

.TP
.B help
Show usage information.

.TP
.B version
Show version information.

.SH RESTORE SEMANTICS
Restore intentionally uses
.B prt-get install
rather than
.BR "prt-get depinst" .
Resolving dependencies again against a newer ports tree could install packages
that were not present in the stored snapshot.

.PP
As a consequence, restore can fail when missing packages need to be installed
in a dependency order different from the order produced by the snapshot
comparison. On failure,
.B prt-snapshot
aborts immediately and does not remove snapshots newer than the requested
target.

.SH SAFETY
Operational commands require root.

.PP
The snapshot directory
.B /var/lib/pkg/snapshots
must be owned by root, must not be a symbolic link, and must not be writable by
group or others.

.PP
Version 0.4 validates snapshot identifiers and package names before destructive
operations, creates snapshots through temporary files, prevents numbered
snapshot collisions, serializes operations with a lock, limits
.B clean
to the numeric snapshot namespace, and preserves newer snapshots when restore
fails.

.SH FILES

.TP
.B /var/lib/pkg/snapshots
Persistent snapshot store.

.TP
.B /var/lib/pkg/snapshots/.lock
Temporary operation lock.

.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns zero on successful completion and non-zero when validation, snapshot
capture, comparison, locking, package installation, package removal, or other
required operations fail.

